{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Field-Based Transfer and Coaching","title":"Technology Observation Notes","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting technology observation notes to a low-inference observation note set with access-distribution evidence and a documented deleted AI-added claim, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"07","section":"03","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-01","statement":"Produce a low-inference technology observation note set a second observer could use to reach an independent conclusion, recording who had access and which AI-added claim was deleted and why"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_technology_observation_notes","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/07/02"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_calibration","cat531-supplement_07_03_lo_03_direct_check"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":3,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_calibration","cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_ai_boundary"],"cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-03":["cat531-supplement_07_03_lo_03_direct_check"],"cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-04":["cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_artifact_signal"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Technology Observation Notes\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Technology Observation Notes, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/07-03-technology-observation-notes.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Technology Observation Notes: Technology Observation Notes, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nWhen you watch a class use a technology, your brain delivers conclusions before it delivers evidence. \"The platform engaged them\" arrives instantly; the timestamps and counts that would justify it do not. Observation notes are the discipline of recording the evidence and holding the conclusion. The difference matters because a conclusion (\"it worked\") cannot be checked by anyone else, while a record (\"6 of 24 students sat on the hint screen for over two minutes before any check-in\") can be read by a second observer who reaches their own judgment.\n\nThis section treats technology observation notes as a record built to be independently verified. The skill is the low-inference description: writing what a camera would have captured, separately from what you concluded, so that the conclusion remains open to revision.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Separate low-inference observation (what happened, with time and count) from high-inference judgment (what it means).\n- Capture access patterns, who used which feature and who received teacher attention, not just whole-class impressions.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move, including an AI-added claim you deleted for lack of evidence.\n- Produce a note set a second observer could use to reach an independent conclusion about the tool's effect.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nThe phrase \"the students were engaged\" feels like an observation. It is a verdict wearing the costume of evidence. Engagement is invisible; what is visible is eyes on screens, hands raised, time on a task, words typed. A note that records the verdict skips the data and locks in a conclusion no one can audit, including you a week later.\n\nThe repair is mechanical: for every conclusion that wants to enter your notes, write instead the observable behavior that produced it. \"Engaged\" becomes \"22 of 24 students typed a response within 90 seconds of the prompt.\" Now a colleague can disagree with your interpretation while still trusting your record, which is exactly the property a professional observation needs.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nObservation notes operate on an inference ladder. At the bottom rung is the raw observable: a count, a timestamp, a quoted student utterance, a recorded seating position. One rung up is the pattern: three students who use read-aloud all sat in the back row. At the top is the judgment: the tool may be creating an access gap. Good notes keep the rungs separate and labeled, so a reader can accept your bottom-rung data while testing your top-rung claim.\n\nThe failure mode is collapsing the ladder, writing the top rung as if it were the bottom. \"The tool improved their learning\" is a top-rung judgment presented as a fact, and it is unfalsifiable in your notes because no data sits beneath it. The cure is to record observables densely and interpretations sparingly, and to mark which is which.\n\nThree lenses shape what you choose to observe:\n\n- **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** directs attention to whether the technology served the content goal or replaced it. Time on the platform's hint screen is a [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge) observable: it can mean productive struggle or a tool that scaffolds badly.\n- **Critical edtech studies** directs attention to access and attention distribution. Who got teacher check-ins, who sat where, which students used assistive features; these are the observables that reveal whether a tool widens or narrows participation.\n- **[AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)** warns that when you ask an AI to summarize raw notes, it will smooth them into flattering generalities (\"highly motivated\") that your data never supported.\n\nA lens has done its work when it changed what you wrote down, not just what you concluded after the fact.\n\n<inline-check question=\"A note reads: 'The platform clearly engaged students.' Which rung of the inference ladder is this?\" explanation=\"This is a top-rung judgment presented as if it were a bottom-rung observable. Engagement is invisible; what is visible is eyes on screens, time on task, words typed. The repair is to demote it to the observable that produced it, such as '22 of 24 students typed a response within 90 seconds.'\" options='[{\"text\":\"A top-rung judgment with no observable beneath it\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A bottom-rung observable a second observer could confirm\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A labeled pattern linking several observables\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare two ways of recording the same five minutes. The high-inference version commits to a verdict:\n\n> The platform clearly engaged students and improved their learning.\n\nNothing in that sentence can be checked. The low-inference version records what a second observer could confirm:\n\n> At 10:14, 6 of 24 students sat on the platform's hint screen for more than two minutes before any teacher check-in. Three students who use read-aloud were seated in the back row and received zero teacher check-ins in 20 minutes.\n\nThe second version makes no claim about engagement or learning, and that restraint is the point. It hands a reader the timestamps, counts, and seating data to reach a conclusion, and it surfaces an access question (the back-row read-aloud students) that the verdict version buried.\n\n### Annotated Judgment Call: Observing an Adaptive Math Platform\n\nPriya observes a 9th-grade class using an adaptive math platform and drafts observation notes.\n\n1. **Before (judgment slipped in early):** \"The platform clearly engaged students and improved their learning.\"\n2. **Rewrite to observable evidence:** \"At 10:14, 6 of 24 students sat on the platform's hint screen for more than two minutes before any teacher check-in.\"\n3. **Access pattern noticed:** Three students who use the read-aloud feature were seated in the back row and received zero teacher check-ins in 20 minutes. Priya records this as an access question, not a verdict.\n4. **AI summary corrected:** She fed her raw notes to an AI tool, which added that students were \"highly motivated.\" Her notes recorded no such evidence, so she deleted the claim.\n5. **After (reviewable note set):** Time-stamped on-task and off-task counts, which students used which features, teacher check-in timing, and the back-row access gap, kept separate from her interpretation.\n\nA second observer could now reach an independent conclusion about the platform's effect on this class.\n\nSort each line below into the correct rung. Observables can be confirmed by a second observer; inferences are your conclusions.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"6 of 24 students sat on the hint screen for over two minutes before any check-in\",\"definition\":\"Observable (count and timestamp a second observer could confirm)\"},{\"term\":\"Three read-aloud students in the back row received zero check-ins in 20 minutes\",\"definition\":\"Observable (attention distribution a second observer could confirm)\"},{\"term\":\"The tool may be creating an access gap\",\"definition\":\"Inference (a labeled judgment held open as a question)\"},{\"term\":\"The platform improved their learning\",\"definition\":\"Inference (a verdict with no observable beneath it)\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\nWalk the demotion move yourself before the reveal shows the reviewable note.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Demoting a verdict to an observable\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You wrote: \\\"The platform clearly engaged students and improved their learning.\\\" Before reading on, what is wrong with logging this as an observation?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"It is too short to be useful\",\"Nothing in it can be checked by a second observer\",\"It should be more positive about the tool\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Correct. Neither engagement nor improved learning is visible. The sentence is a verdict wearing the costume of evidence; no second observer could confirm it.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Demote it to what a camera saw: \\\"At 10:14, 6 of 24 students sat on the platform hint screen for more than two minutes before any teacher check-in.\\\" The restraint is the point; you hand the reader the data to reach their own conclusion.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Add the distribution lens: \\\"Three students who use read-aloud were seated in the back row and received zero teacher check-ins in 20 minutes.\\\" Keep the only judgment (\\\"an access question\\\") explicitly marked as a question, not a finding. The AI summary that called students \\\"highly motivated\\\" is deleted, because no line in the record supports it.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nRecord observables first, patterns second, and judgments last and clearly labeled. When you catch yourself writing a conclusion, demote it: ask what you saw that produced it and write that instead. Priya's note follows the rule; \"engaged and improved learning\" is demoted to the timestamped hint-screen count and the read-aloud seating data, and the only judgment (\"an access question\") is explicitly marked as a question rather than a finding.\n\nBefore you trust an observation note, three checks should pass:\n\n1. **Verifiability check:** Could a second observer in the same room confirm each line, or do some lines record your conclusion rather than the event?\n2. **Distribution check:** Do the notes capture who got attention and who used which feature, or only an undifferentiated whole-class impression?\n3. **Cleanup check:** What did you remove, including any AI-added generality, because no observable in your notes supported it?\n\nIf the notes contain verdicts without underlying observables, they document your impression, not the lesson.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe first misreading is treating engagement as observed when it is inferred; eyes on a screen are visible, engagement is not. The second is averaging the class into a single impression and missing the distribution, which is where access problems live. The third is letting an AI summary launder your notes into confident generalities; when a tool reports students were \"highly motivated\" and no line in your record shows it, the honest move is to delete the claim, not to keep it because it reads well.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the inference ladder to a different observation. You watch a class use a collaborative coding platform. The impulse is to write \"students collaborated well.\" Demote it. What did you see? Maybe: \"In 30 minutes, 4 of 8 pairs alternated who typed; the other 4 had one student type the entire time while the partner watched.\" That is a low-inference record, and it surfaces a participation gap the verdict would have hidden.\n\nNow add the distribution lens: of the four passive partners, three were the only girls in their pairs. That pattern, kept separate from any conclusion, is the observable that matters; whether it reflects the tool, the grouping, or the task is a judgment for later. If an AI summary then tells you \"the class worked well together,\" you delete it, because your own data shows half the pairs did not. The walkthrough repeats the same move every time: write what a camera saw, track who was included, and refuse the conclusion your notes cannot support.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nAs you read observation notes, keep a two-column note: **observable** on the left, **inference** on the right. Sort every line. A note dominated by the right column is an opinion piece; a note with a dense left column and a sparse, clearly labeled right column is evidence. The ratio is the tell.\n\nA strong reader of observation notes can answer four questions:\n\n1. Which lines could a second observer in the room have confirmed, and which are the writer's conclusions?\n2. What does the record show about who received attention and who did not?\n3. Where did the writer hold a judgment open as a question rather than closing it as a finding?\n4. What would change in the notes if the writer had watched the same tool with a different class?\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nAn observation note is strong when handing it to a second observer would let them reach a conclusion you never wrote down. That requires timestamps, counts, named features, and attention distribution on the page, with interpretation kept thin and labeled. Tidy formatting is the last layer; a note that reads cleanly but consists mostly of verdicts has skipped the observable layer that gives it value.\n\nA useful closing line sounds like this: \"I recorded the hint-screen times and the read-aloud seating because those are checkable, and I kept the access concern as a question rather than a verdict. I deleted the AI line about motivation, because looking conclusive is not the same as having seen it.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nTake the same low-inference discipline to a setting you cannot watch live: reviewing a screen-recording of an asynchronous lesson where students used an AI tutor at home. Now you have no seating, no live check-ins, and no room to read. The observables shift to click timestamps, time-on-step, and which hints were opened, while the distribution question becomes which students never reached the later steps. If you can keep the same separation of observable from inference when the data source changes from a live room to a log file, you have the discipline rather than one classroom habit.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the technology observation note set packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/07-03-technology-observation-notes-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"technology observation note set (low-inference records, access distribution, and a deleted AI-added claim)\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"07.03\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the technology observation note set.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the technology observation note set for CAT 531 07.03. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_technology_observation_notes\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that your notes record verifiable observables with times and counts, capture who got attention and who used which feature, label any judgment as a question, and show what AI-added claim you deleted for lack of evidence.\n\n## Concept Map\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching\", \"cat531_supplement_technology_observation_notes\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_technology_observation_notes","stem":"While observing a 9th-grade class using an adaptive math platform, a candidate writes observation notes. Which note is an observation a colleague could verify rather than a judgment slipped in early?","options":["The platform clearly engaged the students and improved their learning.","At 10:14, 6 of 24 students were on the platform's hint screen for more than two minutes before any teacher check-in.","The teacher should have used a better tool for this lesson.","The students seemed to enjoy the technology overall."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An observation note records time-stamped, countable behavior that another observer could confirm. The other options state conclusions or preferences without the observed evidence underneath them.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_judgment_before_observation","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_field_based_transfer_and_coaching","cat531_supplement_technology_observation_notes","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-04"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"The candidate noticed that three students who use the platform's read-aloud feature were seated far from the teacher. Which annotation turns this into useful evidence for a later coaching conversation?","options":["Note that the technology was fine for most students.","Note the pattern: read-aloud users clustered in the back row received zero teacher check-ins in 20 minutes, which is an access question to raise.","Note that the room was a little noisy.","Note that the candidate would have arranged the seats differently."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A strong annotation names a pattern tied to access (who got attention and who did not) so it can drive a coaching conversation. The other notes are vague or are the candidate's preference, not observed evidence.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_notes_miss_access_pattern","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"The candidate feeds raw observation notes into an AI tool and asks it to summarize what happened. The AI adds that students were highly motivated, which the notes never recorded. What should she do?","options":["Keep the motivation claim because the AI summary reads well.","Delete the unsupported motivation claim because the observation notes contain no evidence of it, and keep only what was observed.","Add a survey afterward so the AI claim becomes true.","Ask the AI to make the summary even more positive."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"An observation summary may only assert what the notes recorded. AI-added interpretation that has no observed evidence must be removed, not retrofitted.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_ai_invents_observation","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_technology_observation_notes_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which set of observation notes would let a second observer reach an independent conclusion about the platform's effect on this class?","options":["A single sentence saying the lesson went well.","Time-stamped counts of on-task and off-task moments, which students used which features, teacher check-in timing, and any access gaps, separated from the candidate's interpretation.","A list of features the platform advertises.","The candidate's overall rating of the teacher."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Notes that separate verifiable evidence (counts, timing, feature use, access gaps) from interpretation let another observer judge for themselves. A summary rating or a feature list does not.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_summary_replaces_evidence","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-01","cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531-supplement_07_03_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-07-03-lo-03"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_judgment_before_observation","pattern":"judgment_before_observation","description":"Recording conclusions about engagement or quality before recording the observable behavior","trigger":"candidate writes that a tool engaged students without time-stamped, countable evidence","feedback":"Record what you could see and count first. Save the conclusion until the observed evidence supports it.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_notes_miss_access_pattern","pattern":"notes_miss_access_pattern","description":"Writing observation notes that overlook who got teacher attention and who did not","trigger":"candidate notes overall climate but no pattern of access across students","feedback":"Track who used which feature and who received check-ins. Access patterns are the evidence a coaching conversation needs.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_ai_invents_observation","pattern":"ai_invents_observation","description":"Keeping AI-added claims (such as motivation) that the raw observation notes never recorded","trigger":"candidate accepts an AI summary that asserts more than the notes contain","feedback":"An observation summary may only state what you recorded. Delete AI interpretation that has no observed evidence.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_7_3_summary_replaces_evidence","pattern":"summary_replaces_evidence","description":"Offering an overall rating in place of verifiable evidence a second observer could check","trigger":"candidate submits a one-line verdict or a feature list instead of observed data","feedback":"Keep counts, timing, feature use, and access gaps separate from your interpretation so another observer can judge independently.","rail_action":"represent"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"d77c0194e31f89f15277b4e2647d772ebec47c8b384e882d56ce77699eae3fdf","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}